<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden Horn</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Haliç</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golden Horn is an inlet of the Bosporus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> that forms a deep natural harbour between the old city and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Galata</a>. It sheltered the shipping of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and could be closed by a great chain in times of siege. The Genoese colony of Pera stood on its northern shore.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-horn.jpg" alt="Golden Horn" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kaidor (Russian original), Redx360 (English translation) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ottoman_Istanbul_-_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Golden Horn, Altın Boynuz or Haliç in Turkish, is the estuary of the Alibey and Kağıthane Creeks. It is 7.5 kilometres long and 750 metres across at its widest, and about 35 metres deep where it flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>. Its waters mark the northern boundary of the peninsula of old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and separate the historic centre from the rest of the city. Its name is understood to refer to its horn-shaped outline. Historians think that the word golden refers either to the riches brought in through its harbour or to the yellow light on the water at sunset. The Turkish name means estuary and derives from the Arabic khaleej, a gulf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds show a significant urban presence around the Golden Horn from at least the 7th century BCE, with smaller settlements back to 6700 BCE, from ancient ports and ships found during construction of the Yenikapı subway station and the Marmaray tunnel. The deep harbour was an economic and military asset, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> founded Nova Roma, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, on the existing city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> for the same reason. The Eastern Roman Empire kept its naval headquarters there, and walls were built along the shore against attack from the sea.</p><p>A large chain was drawn across the entrance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to the old tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Galata</a>, called the Megàlos Pyrgos by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. It was broken or circumvented three times. In the 10th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> dragged their longships around Galata and relaunched them in the Horn, and the Byzantines defeated them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a>. In 1204, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, Venetian ships broke the chain with a ram, and the Latin Crusaders largely destroyed the tower. In 1453 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, having failed to break the chain by force, towed his ships over greased logs past Galata into the estuary. The Genoese built a new tower in 1348, now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata-tower">Galata Tower</a>.</p><p>After the conquest of 1453 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> resettled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in the Phanar, today's Fener. Balat had been inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the Byzantine age, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> invited the Jews expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to settle there. In 1502 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> drew a single-span bridge of 240 metres over the Horn for Bayezid II, which was never built. Mehmed II founded the Haliç Shipyard in 1455, which worked into the 1960s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Both shores of the Golden Horn are now settled, with parks, museums, university campuses, naval facilities and Muslim, Jewish and Christian cemeteries. The inlet is crossed by bridges including the Galata Bridge and the Atatürk Bridge, and ferries link its suburbs. Industrial waste polluted it until the 1980s, and it was later cleaned in two phases.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Golden Horn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The land walls held, and the Slav boat fleet was destroyed in the <b>Golden Horn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The colonists, perhaps joined by settlers from other Greek cities, occupied the triangular promontory between the <b>Golden Horn</b>, the Bosporus and the Sea of Marmara, with their acropolis on the site of the later Topkapı Palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Süleymaniye Mosque stands on the Third Hill of Istanbul, above the <b>Golden Horn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Nymphaeum of 1261 rewarded Genoa for helping Michael VIII Palaiologos, and in 1267 the emperor granted the Genoese the suburb of Pera, or Galata, on the north side of the <b>Golden Horn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-pera">Genoese Pera</a></li></ul></section>
